                                         To Undo LVM configuation
*************************************************************************************************************
Note : Need to umount LVM prior to execuation of below steps. 
1. To delete LV:
     command to check LV : lvs 
     command to delete LV: lvremove /dev/datavg/datavol1  
                  here: /dev/datavg/datavol1 is logical volume name (lv name )
2. To delete VG 
      command to check VG: vgs 
      command to delete VG: vgremove  /dev/datavg 
                      here /dev/datavg is VG name 
3. To delete PV 
      command to check PV: pvs 
      command to delete PV: pvremove /dev/xvdf
                  here /dev/xvdf is PV name. 

 To mount LVM permanently , need to update /etc/fstab
 for eg : /dev/mapper/fedora_localhost--live-root /mnt/fedora/root ext4 defaults 0 0
         /dev/mapper/fedora_localhost--live-home /mnt/fedora/home ext4 defaults 0 0 

*************************************************************************************************************
                                         To mount EBS volume in linux
*************************************************************************************************************

1. Create an XFS filesystem using the EBS volume.
   
sudo mkfs -t xfs /dev/xvdf

2. Create the directory which you want to mount.
  sudo mkdir /data

3. Mount the newly created directory.

sudo mount /dev/xvdf /data

4. The above step will only mount the volume temporarily & will not survive a reboot. To make it permanent, we need to add an entry in /etc/fstab file. Before, that we need to find the Volume ID. This command will show this information.
 sudo blkid

5. Copy the existing /etc/fstab file. 

sudo cp /etc/fstab /etc/fstab.bkp

6. Add the below line to /etc/fstab file & save it. 
UUID=aebf131c-6957-451e-8d34-ec978d9581ae  /data  xfs  defaults,nofail  0  2

Note : Replace UUID with the actual value from step # 4.

7. To test, first unmount the volume. 

sudo unmount /data

8. Mount it back.

sudo mount -a

9. Reboot , then check the output of df -h to verify EBS volume is still mounted or not. 

